{% endfor %}

## Before you begin

- **Amazon Quick on desktop.** Skills and local connectors are managed under
  **Customize**, and the skills' scripts run with Quick's **Code Execution**
  system tool. Turn that tool on under **Customize > Connectors**.
{% if mcp_url %}
- A Tableau Cloud site with the Tableau MCP server enabled.
- Quick support for OAuth **Client ID Metadata Documents (CIMD)**. Tableau's
  sign-in registers clients only through CIMD, not Dynamic Client
  Registration. (VERIFY: AWS has said this is coming; until it ships, the
  connector fails at sign-in.)
{% else %}
- A local [tableau-mcp](https://github.com/tableau/tableau-mcp) checkout,
  built with `npm run build`, whose `.env` sets `SERVER`, `SITE_NAME` and auth.
{% endif %}

## 1. Add the Tableau connector

{% if mcp_url %}
**Option A: import the config.** In Quick, open **Customize > Connectors**,
choose **Create**, then **Create local connector**, then **Import**, and enter
the path to `connector/mcp.json`. It points at `{{ mcp_url }}`. (VERIFY that an
imported remote server signs in with OAuth; the import's **Remote** form
otherwise takes only a bearer token.)

**Option B: create a cloud connector.** Choose **Create**, then **Cloud
Connector**, then **Model Context Protocol (MCP)**, and enter:

- **Name:** `{{ server_key }}`
- **MCP server endpoint:** `{{ mcp_url }}`
- **Authentication:** user authentication. Quick finds Tableau's
  authorization server from the endpoint, so no client ID or secret is needed.

Sign in to Tableau when asked. Each user signs in with their own account, so
they see only what their Tableau permissions allow. Quick registers at most
100 tools per connector; after the Tableau MCP server adds tools, choose
**Sync** on the connector to pick them up.
{% else %}
In Quick, open **Customize > Connectors**, choose **Create**, then **Create
local connector**, then **Import**, and enter the path to
`connector/mcp.json`. It runs your tableau-mcp checkout over stdio: set
`TABLEAU_MCP_DIR` in this repo's `.env` before building, or in the connector's
environment variables. The connector works only while this machine is on.
{% endif %}

## 2. Add the skills

Open **Customize > Skills**, choose **Create**, then **From file**, and select
`tableau-skills.zip`. (VERIFY that one zip installs every skill in it. If
Quick takes one skill per upload, choose **From folder** instead and add each
folder under `skills/`.) Review each skill, then **Publish** it.

To share the skills with your team, choose **Share** on each one. People you
share with also need the connector.

## Try it

- `What can you do with Tableau?`
- `Show me the regional sales dashboard.`
- `Explain the largest trends in this view.`
- `Advise me on how to design an executive dashboard for regional sales performance.`
- `Review this dashboard and prioritize its three highest-impact improvements.`
- `Run a metadata quality scan on the Sales datasource.`

## Updating

Rebuild with `python3 build/build.py quick`, then open each skill, choose
**Edit**, replace its files with the new folder, and **Publish**. Everyone you
shared it with moves to the new version.
